# printer-interrupt-sweep.ti58c
#
# Where does the printer interrupt cut a dot row? A sweep for the real
# TI-58C + PC-100A: 89 interrupted lines, each cut about 16 instruction
# cycles later than the one before, from 1905 to 3137 cycles after the
# print starts. A dot row lasts 508 cycles, so the sweep covers the end
# of row 4, rows 5 and 6 and the start of row 7 (counting from 1), about
# 32 lines per row. Photographed, it shows when each column of a row is
# fired, how long it is heated, and where a row starts relative to the
# print.
#
# THE PRINTOUT
#
# For each k = 11 … 99:
#   k            Prt, a complete line: the number of the test
#   E4W, E4W,…   the test line, cut short by the interrupt
#   (blank)      Adv
# The cut line shows its first rows complete and the row being printed
# at the cut partly. Every column of rows 4–7 has a dot in at least one
# of E, 4, W and the comma, and row 5 of "4" and row 7 of "E" are full
# rows, so each column shows whether it was fired.
#
# WHY NOT NOPS
#
# A Nop costs 224 cycles, almost half a row; one column takes about 60.
# The delay comes from Int instead: Int of 10^-k is 0, and Int takes
# about 16 cycles longer per step of k (for k ≥ 11). The program computes
# 10^-k itself, so nothing has to be keyed in per line.
#
# Cut times: k, instruction cycles from the PRT.GO of Op 05 to the
# PRT.GO of the 1F (emulator, TI-58C ROM, which the real unit runs
# unchanged), and the same in rows of 508 cycles. The fraction is the
# point within the row if rows start right at PRT.GO; finding the real
# start is one aim of the sweep. Pairs with the same time (18/19, 20/21,
# …) are repeats of each other.
#  11 1905 3.75  12 1921 3.78  13 1937 3.81  14 1953 3.84  15 1969 3.88
#  16 1985 3.91  17 2001 3.94  18 2017 3.97  19 2017 3.97  20 2049 4.03
#  21 2049 4.03  22 2065 4.06  23 2081 4.10  24 2097 4.13  25 2113 4.16
#  26 2129 4.19  27 2129 4.19  28 2145 4.22  29 2161 4.25  30 2193 4.32
#  31 2193 4.32  32 2209 4.35  33 2225 4.38  34 2241 4.41  35 2241 4.41
#  36 2257 4.44  37 2273 4.47  38 2289 4.51  39 2305 4.54  40 2337 4.60
#  41 2337 4.60  42 2353 4.63  43 2353 4.63  44 2369 4.66  45 2385 4.69
#  46 2401 4.73  47 2417 4.76  48 2433 4.79  49 2449 4.82  50 2481 4.88
#  51 2465 4.85  52 2481 4.88  53 2497 4.92  54 2513 4.95  55 2529 4.98
#  56 2545 5.01  57 2561 5.04  58 2577 5.07  59 2577 5.07  60 2593 5.10
#  61 2609 5.14  62 2625 5.17  63 2641 5.20  64 2657 5.23  65 2673 5.26
#  66 2689 5.29  67 2689 5.29  68 2705 5.32  69 2721 5.36  70 2737 5.39
#  71 2753 5.42  72 2769 5.45  73 2785 5.48  74 2801 5.51  75 2801 5.51
#  76 2817 5.55  77 2833 5.58  78 2849 5.61  79 2865 5.64  80 2881 5.67
#  81 2897 5.70  82 2913 5.73  83 2913 5.73  84 2929 5.77  85 2945 5.80
#  86 2961 5.83  87 2977 5.86  88 2993 5.89  89 3009 5.92  90 3025 5.95
#  91 3025 5.95  92 3041 5.99  93 3057 6.02  94 3073 6.05  95 3089 6.08
#  96 3105 6.11  97 3121 6.14  98 3137 6.18  99 3137 6.18
#
# KEYING IT IN (TI-58C, default partition 239.29)
#
# 1. LRN, key the PROGRAM below (steps 000–049), LRN.
# 2. Make the 1F at 008 (the fast-mode hack of printer-interrupt.ti59):
#      1 + 48 EE 12 +/- =     X := 1.000000000048
#      GTO 012  SST  0  STO   Prg Src := 8, the run stops at 007
#      LRN  2nd Ins  LRN      008 := 1F, 009 := RTN, 016 on move up one
#      CLR  RST
#    The constant memory keeps the 1F: it has to be made only once.
# 3. 11 STO 01   1705435700 STO 02   100 STO 03
# 4. GTO 017  R/S
# R01 is the next k and R03 one past the last: 40 STO 01  61 STO 03
# GTO 017 R/S repeats k = 40 … 60.
#
# THE CLOCK DRIFT
#
# The cut time counts instruction cycles of the calculator, and the
# printer runs on the calculator's clock. A drifting clock changes the
# length of a cycle, not the number of cycles, so the cut point within a
# row stays where it is. What can change is the heat a dot gets: at a
# slower clock every heating pulse lasts longer in milliseconds, so a
# half-heated dot may come out a little darker.
#
# WHY THE PROGRAM LOOKS LIKE THIS
#
# - 000 INV SBR, 008 1F, 009 RTN: the interrupt continues at step 000,
#   which returns to 009, which returns from the SBR 005 (as in
#   printer-interrupt.ti59).
# - No labels: the 1F stops every label search that reaches it, so a
#   label after 008 can never be found. The driver uses SBR 005 and a
#   branch to 017 instead.
# - Op 05 Int 1F: Op 05 starts the line, Int of 10^-k is the delay, and
#   leaves the 0 that the 1F needs. The 0 must be a result. A 0 being
#   keyed in, or CLR just before the 1F, makes the interrupt hang.
# - Steps 001–004 are Nops so that the 1F stays at 008; they run before
#   the print and don't change the delay.

MODEL: TI-58C
PRINTER: on
PARTITION: 239.29

PROGRAM:
000  92  INV SBR   # the interrupt continues here: return to 009
001  68  Nop
002  68  Nop
003  68  Nop
004  68  Nop
005  69  Op        # SBR 005 enters here
006  05  05        # start printing the line …
007  59  Int       # … Int(10^-k) = 0, its time is the delay
008  23  lnx       # becomes 1F: the interrupt
009  53  (         # becomes 92 RTN
010  00  0
011  00  0
012  86  St flg    # hack trigger (overwritten by the Ins)
013  00  0         # swallowed by the hack
014  00  0         # makes X displayable before the R/S
015  91  R/S       # stops the hack's run (moves to 016)
# From here on, each step moves up one during the Ins: 016 runs as 017.
016  43  RCL       # 017: start of the loop
017  01  01
018  99  Prt       # print k
019  43  RCL
020  02  02        # E4W,  E4W,  E4W,  E4W,
021  69  Op
022  01  01
023  69  Op
024  02  02
025  69  Op
026  03  03
027  69  Op
028  04  04
029  43  RCL
030  01  01
031  94  +/-
032  22  INV
033  28  log       # 10^-k
034  71  SBR
035  00  0
036  05  05        # SBR 005: the interrupted line
037  98  Adv
038  69  Op
039  21  21        # k := k + 1
040  43  RCL
041  03  03
042  32  x<>t      # t := end
043  43  RCL
044  01  01
045  22  INV
046  77  x>=t
047  00  0
048  17  17        # k < end: next line (017)
049  91  R/S

REGISTERS:
01 = 11            # first k
02 = 1705435700    # print codes E 4 W , space
03 = 100           # one past the last k

KEYSTROKES:
82          # 1
85          # +
72          # 4
63          # 8
52          # EE
82          # 1
83          # 2
94          # +/-
95          # =      X = 1.000000000048
61          # GTO
92          # 0
82          # 1
83          # 2      → step 012
41          # SST    St flg executes, operand pending
92          # 0      first operand digit
42          # STO    ends it: Prg Src 8, run stops at 007
31          # LRN    008, buffer loaded from ROM row 17
21 41       # Ins    008 := 1F, 009 := RTN
31          # LRN
25          # CLR
81          # RST    Prg Src 0
61          # GTO
92          # 0
82          # 1
62          # 7      → step 017
91          # R/S    the sweep
